The function $ f(n) = 3^{2^n} $ grows exponentially with a double exponential term, while $ g(n) = n! \times n^3 $ involves a factorial multiplied by a polynomial. Using Stirling's approximation, $ \ln(n!) \approx n \ln n - n $, so $ \ln(g(n)) \approx n \ln n $. In contrast, $ \ln(f(n)) = 2^n \ln 3 $, which grows exponentially faster than $ n \ln n $. Thus, $ f(n) $ dominates $ g(n) $ as $ n \to \infty $.

**Final Answer**
\boxed{f(n)}

